B4.1 -- what the dispersion does to the z-score bimodality_sigma reports
==============================================================================
  bimodality_sigma(i) = (far_val[i] - floor[i]) / (sqrt(2) * FINE_FOCUS_NOISE_SD).
  The numerator is a per-pixel difference of fine responses; the denominator
  is a global constant. If the true per-pixel SD is rho(p) * the constant,
  the reported z is inflated by exactly 1/rho(p).

  stack             rho_obs     p05     p95 |  z inflation at p05    at p95   ratio
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  coffee-bubbles     1.7494  0.9843  3.9229 |               1.016     0.255   3.986x
  109MSDCF           1.2430  0.8918  1.6766 |               1.121     0.596   1.880x
  119MSDCF           1.2688  0.8755  1.7684 |               1.142     0.565   2.020x
  113MSDCF           1.2297  0.8818  1.6787 |               1.134     0.596   1.904x
  112MSDCF           1.2836  0.9150  1.7730 |               1.093     0.564   1.938x
  foam               1.4794  0.9234  2.5550 |               1.083     0.391   2.767x
  coffee-bean        1.0856  0.8027  1.4477 |               1.246     0.691   1.804x
  wrapped-text-2     1.3492  0.9396  1.9263 |               1.064     0.519   2.050x
  wrapped-text-1     1.1429  0.8288  1.4876 |               1.207     0.672   1.795x
  bee-1              1.1392  0.8487  1.4746 |               1.178     0.678   1.737x
  bee-2              1.1797  0.8865  1.5102 |               1.128     0.662   1.703x
  bee-3              1.1213  0.8328  1.4597 |               1.201     0.685   1.753x

  Within a single stack the same reported z means between 0.255
  and 1.246 true sigma depending only on where the pixel is.
  Worst within-stack spread 3.986x, best 1.703x.
==============================================================================
B4.2 -- does the per-stack placebo cut absorb it?
==============================================================================
  merge_cmd.rs sets bimodal_cut = p99 of the PLACEBO population, one scalar
  for the whole image, and flags pixels with b >= cut. The placebo is a
  frame-permutation of the SAME pixels, so it carries the same per-pixel
  rho(p). Two questions, and they have different answers.

  (a) Does it absorb the STACK-level dispersion?  YES, by construction:
      corpus rho_obs spans 1.612x across stacks, and a cut
      re-derived per stack from that stack's own placebo sees the stack's
      own scale. The stack-level limb of this defect is already handled.

  (b) Does it absorb the WITHIN-image dispersion?  NO. The cut is a single
      scalar over pixels. Under a global p99 cut the per-pixel false-positive
      rate is not 1% everywhere; it is concentrated in the high-rho tail.

  stack             p95/p05     implied FPR ratio, p95 pixel vs p05 pixel
  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  coffee-bubbles      3.986                   7.3x  vs           0.000x
  109MSDCF            1.880                   3.7x  vs           0.040x
  119MSDCF            2.020                   4.1x  vs           0.022x
  113MSDCF            1.904                   3.8x  vs           0.037x
  112MSDCF            1.938                   3.9x  vs           0.032x
  foam                2.767                   5.7x  vs           0.001x
  coffee-bean         1.804                   3.5x  vs           0.056x
  wrapped-text-2      2.050                   4.2x  vs           0.019x
  wrapped-text-1      1.795                   3.5x  vs           0.058x
  bee-1               1.737                   3.3x  vs           0.073x
  bee-2               1.703                   3.2x  vs           0.083x
  bee-3               1.753                   3.4x  vs           0.068x

  ILLUSTRATION ONLY, and labelled so: bimodality_sigma's null is a
  multiplicity tail over n-3 frames, not a Gaussian tail (through_focus.rs:731
  says so, and M63 section 2.3 registered the same point and refused to quote
  a sigma-count for exactly this reason). The Gaussian numbers above are a
  scale illustration of a real effect, NOT a false-positive rate.
